# Per-scenario rubric for the LLM judge layer.
#
# Four criteria per step, weights summing to 1.0: map_quality 0.40 / specificity 0.25
# / justification 0.20 / uncertainty 0.15.
#
# CYCLE 3 on chatwoot. Supersedes the cycle-2 rubric, kept at
# scenarios/chatwoot.rubric.yaml.never-names-it-cycle2.bak, whose step-2 criteria rewarded
# an audit restricted to code that never writes the record's class name. That restriction
# was met by a baseline running one grep per named hiding mechanism, scoring 12 of 16 - so
# "does not write the name" is dropped as the discriminating demand and does not appear
# below.
#
# WHAT REPLACES IT: the demand is now a two-link CALL PATH per place - the caller, the
# go-between it hands the work to, and the location of the hand-off. An entry that names
# a place but cannot say what it hands off to has not answered the question asked, however
# real the dependency is. That is what step 2's map_quality and specificity score.
#
# WHY step 2 penalises the direct ring: the maintainer explicitly excluded the code that
# deals with the record head-on, because that code is what a name search returns and the
# rework already sees it. Re-listing it is answering a different question, and padding
# with it should pull map_quality down rather than leave it flat.
#
# TWO steps, matching the two-step probe. Step names MUST match scenarios/chatwoot.yaml
# exactly (the judge pairs by name). Step 1 is unchanged from cycle 2 by design: one
# variable is held fixed across the re-question.

audience: 'An AI coding agent that will use this answer to safely rework the Chatwoot
  conversation record - the object the whole product is organised around, that a customer''s
  back-and-forth with the support team is filed under. Score for that agent, not a human
  reader and not a docs page. A perfect answer leaves the agent able to change that class
  knowing two things: how the system gets hold of the right one of these when a reply
  arrives from outside and all it holds are the mail headers - traced through the routines
  that make that path up - and then, separately, which code would change behaviour under
  the rework even though nothing on its own line goes near the record. That second part is
  the hard half and it is deliberately narrow. It is not the code that deals with the
  record head-on: that code says the record''s name out loud, a search returns it, and the
  maintainer said they already have it. It is the ring one step outside - code that reaches
  the record only at second hand, by handing the work off to some other object, where it is
  that object and not this code that touches the record. So each entry there is a small
  path: a caller, the go-between it hands to, and the exact file:line where the hand-off is
  made, plus what that place would start getting wrong. Those callers sit scattered across
  the application tree and a second application tree carrying the paid edition, in actions,
  controllers for the dashboard and for the embedded widget, model concerns, channel
  message builders, notification building and push presenters. A weak answer re-lists the
  code that deals with the record directly - the finder strategies, the query builders, the
  services that write to it - because a name search returns them, and that is exactly what
  was excluded; or names a caller but cannot say what it hands the work to, which leaves
  the path unverified; or gives a bare filename; or gives file:line with nothing said about
  what would break, which leaves the agent knowing where to look and not what its change
  would do.

  '
steps:
- name: Map the conversation-record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'Is the inbound-mail matching path traced end to end as one connected path,

        rather than listed as places the class is mentioned? A strong answer reaches the

        routing decision that admits an arriving message to this path, the piece that runs

        the candidate matches in a declared order and stops at the first hit, each separate

        attempt and the identifier it keys off, the fallback that prepares a fresh record

        when none matched, and the point where a merely prepared record is made permanent.

        Credit is for showing how they connect and in what order, not for the count. Could

        the next step act from this map with small targeted lookups rather than a fresh

        exploration?

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does every place carry an exact file:line AND the name of the routine it lives

        in? A bare filename is not specific. A file:line with no routine named around it

        is not specific - the maintainer asked whose code it is, not where the character

        offset falls. A routine named without the file:line where it is defined is not

        specific either.

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each place carry one phrase on what it contributes to the matching path -

        which identifier it keys off, what it scopes the match by, what it falls back to,

        why it refuses? Do the closing sentences say what a rework must preserve in terms

        of behaviour, rather than restating the method names?

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer separate what it verified by reading from what it inferred from a

        name? Guessing at a fallback it never opened is worse than saying it did not

        check. Is completeness claimed without saying how it was established?

        '
- name: Audit every dependent of the conversation-record contract
  criteria:
    map_quality:
      weight: 0.4
      question: 'THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it is about a RING, not a count. The maintainer asked

        only for code that reaches the record at second hand - code whose own line hands

        the work to another object, where it is that object and not this code that touches

        the record. Does the audit sit in that ring? A strong answer is dense in callers of

        the record''s handlers: the endpoint or action that invokes a service and lets the

        service do the writing; the concern mixed into the record that passes itself out to

        a service or a job; the channel builder whose routine calls out to a scope and a

        builder rather than querying anything; the notification path that defers the access

        decision to a policy object; the presenter payload whose every key is forwarded

        through a wrapper. A weak answer re-lists the code that deals with the record

        head-on - the lookup strategies, the query builders, the services and jobs that

        write to it - because that is what a name search returns, and it is exactly what

        was excluded. Padding the list with excluded entries is worse than a shorter list

        that stays in the ring, and should pull this criterion down. Does the answer reach

        BEYOND the main application tree into the second application tree carrying the paid

        edition, and across actions, dashboard and widget controllers, model concerns,

        channel builders and presenters, or is it dense in one kind of place and silent

        about the rest? Areas it could not finish should be declared unfinished rather than

        quietly summarised.

        '
    specificity:
      weight: 0.25
      question: 'Does EVERY place carry its own exact file:line AND the name of what it hands the

        work to? Both, because the entry is a path and half a path locates nothing. A bare

        filename does not count - the whole difficulty here is that the record''s name is

        not on the line, so a filename alone leaves the agent to find it again. A file:line

        with no go-between named is an assertion the agent cannot check, and should be

        scored weaker than one that names the object the work is handed to. Several places

        named together on one line as a set that shares a go-between is the failure this

        criterion exists to catch: however correct the shared reason is, it is one item,

        not a set of located dependents, and it should be scored as one.

        '
    justification:
      weight: 0.2
      question: 'Does each place carry one phrase on what it would start getting wrong if the

        record changed under it? The consequence must be about that place''s own job - the

        agent who stops being assigned, the activity message that stops being posted, the

        push payload that loses a field, the attachment list an agent should not see, the

        merged customer whose history is left behind, the notification that stops being

        gated, the inbound message filed onto the wrong thread - not a restatement of the

        line of code, and not a restatement of the hand-off. Does the answer make the path

        legible, so the agent can see WHY a search for the record''s name would not have

        found this place?

        '
    uncertainty:
      weight: 0.15
      question: 'Does the answer say how it convinced itself this audit is exhaustive, given that

        what it is looking for is defined by a link it has to traverse backwards rather than

        by a name it can search for? An unqualified claim of completeness is overconfidence

        here more than anywhere. Naming a go-between whose callers it knows it did not work

        to the end, and what it expects is still there, is worth more than a confident list

        that quietly stops.

        '
